  "description": "Muzan-e (無残絵), also known as \"Bloody Prints\", refers to Japanese woodcut prints of violent nature published in the late Edo and Meiji periods.\n\nOne of the earliest and most well-known examples is the collection Twenty-Eight Famous Murders with Verse (英名二十八衆句, eimei nijūhasshūku) by the artists Yoshitoshi and Yoshiiku from the 1860s, which depicted several gruesome acts of murder or torture based on historical events or scenes in Kabuki plays.",
